# KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

Sequential, explicit code is **not** a license to add helpers, regex, parsers, or config. Prefer the simplest code that meets the requirement.

Style-guide named steps still apply. Extract a method only when it is a **real business step** (create a lead, find a duplicate), not when it only trims, lowercases, or strips punctuation.

## Do

- Use `trim`, `strtolower`, `str_starts_with`, and `str_replace` instead of regex
- A tiny helper is OK only when the same cleanup is used in several places (e.g. lowercase candidate and existing client)
- Hardcode values that will not change at runtime (prompt paths, headings); change them in git
- Pass structured-output / Form Request fields through unless a real business rule rejects them
- Keep SSRF / auth / validation guards that exist for safety, not for "cleanliness"

## Do not

- Add `normalize*`, `sanitize*`, `nullableString`, or `stringList` helpers that only trim, cast, or regex-clean
- Parse markdown, prompts, or config with custom scanners when reading the whole file is enough
- Add config keys, interfaces, or adapters for a single hardcoded implementation
- Collapse whitespace, strip punctuation, or validate email a second time after the schema / Form Request already did
- Use regex when a string function is enough

## Examples

```php
// BAD — regex "normalization" for a case-insensitive name match
$normalized = preg_replace('/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/u', '', Str::lower(trim($name)));

// GOOD
$companyName = strtolower(trim($name));
```

```php
// BAD — helper used once, only trims
private function normalizeQualificationNotes(mixed $notes): ?string { ... }

// GOOD
$notes = $lead['why_good_fit'] ?? null;
```

```php
// BAD — parse a heading out of an approved prompt file
foreach ($lines as $index => $line) { ... }

// GOOD
$prompt = trim((string) File::get($path));
```
